course: dictionary
lesson: 3882
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The word freeze is one of them. To know which meaning the writer wants, look at the words around it. These nearby words are the context, and they give you clues.
explain: «Much of Earth's fresh water is frozen in ice sheets, huge sheets of ice covering land.» Here frozen means turned into solid ice because it is very cold. The clues are ice sheets and covering land.
explain: «A dog warns before it bites: it yawns, licks its lips, turns its head away, and then freezes and growls.» Here freeze means to stop moving suddenly and stay still. The clue is the list of actions before it, which show the dog is getting ready to act.
explain: «If a card is lost, the bank can freeze it at once, so the money in the account stays safe.» Here freeze means to stop the card from being used. Check it by swapping in block: «the bank can block it» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Freeze can be a verb (freeze, froze, frozen, freezing) or a noun (a freeze).
fact: Water freezes at 0 °C (32 °F), and it gets bigger when it turns to ice. This is why bottles can crack and pipes can burst.
fact: Freezing is the change from liquid to solid, and it is the opposite of melting.
fact: Frozen ponds may have thin ice underneath, so stay off them.
fact: «Freezing cold» means very cold. «Brain freeze» is the sudden headache you get from eating something very cold.
exercise: «Ice or frozen peas should be wrapped in a cloth before you use them.» What does frozen mean here? | *Made solid because it is very cold | Cooked in hot oil | Covered in soft paint | Left out in the sun
exercise: At what temperature does water freeze? | *0 °C (32 °F) | 10 °C (50 °F) | 100 °C (212 °F) | 50 °C (122 °F)
exercise: «The screen froze, so I could not type.» What does froze mean? | Melted into water | Became very warm | *Stopped working | Grew bigger and brighter
test: Which meaning of freeze fits «I'm freezing!»? | To melt into water | *To feel very cold | To stop a bank card from being used | To stop moving suddenly
test: «Freeze!» is shouted. What should you do? | Lie down and sleep | *Stop moving and stay still | Run as fast as you can | Shout your name
exam: «Freezing is the change from liquid to solid, the opposite of melting.» What is the opposite of freezing? | *Melting | Boiling | Cracking | Drying
